==English==
[[File:Bunks in a Seven-Cent Lodging-House, Pell Street.jpeg|thumb|upright|Bunks in a '''flophouse''', circa 1890.]]

===Alternative forms===
* [[flop-house]]

===Etymology===
From {{compound|flop|house|lang=en}}, originally [[hobo]] slang, presumably from slang {{term|flop||lie down to sleep|lang=en}}.<ref name="oetyld">{{R:Online Etymology Dictionary}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
*{{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈflɒphaʊs/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{context|US|slang|lang=en}} A [[cheap]] [[hotel]] or [[boarding house]] where many people sleep in large rooms. {{defdate|from 20th c.}}
#* '''1904''', ''[[w:McClure's|McClure’s Magazine]],'' November 1904:<ref name="oetyld" />
#*: In one of [Cincinnati’s] slum districts stands the Silver Moon, a “'''flop house'''” (i.e., a house where the occupants are “flopped” out of their hanging bunks by letting down the ropes).
#*'''1982''', {{w|TC Boyle}}, ''Water Music'', Penguin 2006, p. 34:
#*:He was born out back of a twopenny '''flophouse''' in what the wags called “The Holy Land” […].

